<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Culture &amp; Lifestyle</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-cul</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-cul" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1441?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-cul" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Controversial kinship: From colonial rule to the Holocaust?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ac319428a892326a24fe01033ef0e2953cf4fda1f74391567caad1f287adfbaf</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T15:49:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228--16129c6452e2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Hitler once called Eastern Europe "Germany's India." A new exhibition examines the links between colonialism and Nazism, and the bitter debate over where comparisons illuminate history and where they fall short.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>'Never Again!': Holocaust remembrance under pressure</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:dab1ff746c1b38a606cc98487fd36a99e35fb81a8a3ba99ee9203e650c712650</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T09:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894--861f4756e8c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Only a few Holocaust survivors remain who can share their stories firsthand. Meanwhile, voices calling for an end to what they describe as a "cult of guilt" are growing louder. How can Holocaust remembrance survive?</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>When colonial Germany committed genocide in Namibia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d8ed65c653e980a7be765072387f9568ef178a28a5a27e7916c94f80b9fa0097</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2024-04-01T11:03:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/when-colonial-germany-committed-genocide-in-namibia/a-67623302--0b863ce528f5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/when-colonial-germany-committed-genocide-in-namibia/a-67623302?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Between 1904 and 1908, Germany's colonial forces reacted to a rebellion by Ovaherero and Nama people in Namibia with brutal violence and prison camps. The trauma and political repercussion remain unresolved.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>37 years after fall of Berlin Wall: How divided are Germans?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cf45f7afab90ccd470ea65b656e6f14f1b773caa16359f36c72ef96e3e015b17</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T13:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/37-years-after-fall-of-berlin-wall-how-divided-are-germans/a-79877461--471b79fce1fe.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/37-years-after-fall-of-berlin-wall-how-divided-are-germans/a-79877461?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Three artists share their thoughts on the reunification of East and West Germany. How do they perceive a shared "we" and what gives them cause for optimism?</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>How Berlin's Arab queers are building their community</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:20cb7b2c76d96c0828e2e1f315525f940ebf1521f71b37e47f56fe0e6f59f582</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-03T14:37:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/how-berlin-s-arab-queers-are-building-their-community/a-79833143--c676c7f349f7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/how-berlin-s-arab-queers-are-building-their-community/a-79833143?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">As the co-founder of the ADIRA Drag Festival, Lebanese performance artist, drag performer, DJ and cultural organizer Hassandra has become an essential part of Berlin's queer artistic landscape.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Harry Potter films turns 25 ahead of new TV series' release</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a2a28e46ef077a74a2f8358ef744babfe5f66970c08f9c8add69c1b5a8149cb7</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T15:14:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/harry-potter-films-turns-25-ahead-of-new-tv-series-release/a-79856130--db1dcb8d550c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/harry-potter-films-turns-25-ahead-of-new-tv-series-release/a-79856130?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">In 2001, "Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone" hit cinemas and took the world by storm. Today, anticipation for the new series is high, but its creator, J.K. Rowling, remains a controversial figure.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Germany's celebrated children's author Janosch has died</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:851f40fa533dbae624b4d74030291303fbd260df8cd22553254c9ffe0c1a06f5</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-04T10:16:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-s-celebrated-children-s-author-janosch-has-died/a-79851600--a3fdc8a2501b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-s-celebrated-children-s-author-janosch-has-died/a-79851600?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Bear, Tiger and Tiger-Duck: The author and illustrator Janosch achieved world fame with his heartwarming children's figures. He died at the age of 95.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Bollywood and the underworld — together in the spotlight?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cfd0071e533465f985333925a8b82f1b27d480fde114095b78606433291fe34a</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-10-31T07:26:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/bollywood-and-the-underworld-together-in-the-spotlight/a-79806129--e6b957cc58ac.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/bollywood-and-the-underworld-together-in-the-spotlight/a-79806129?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A wave of shootings and extortion threats has rattled India's film industry. This time, those claiming responsibility are very different from the Mumbai underworld that had Bollywood in its grip back in the 1990s.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>Brutal, revolutionary empire builder: Genghis Khan's legacy</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2595d20ee84f613552fa4b706a83fa3e140b1e1976992ca08d7da96e435a0811</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-10-20T08:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/brutal-revolutionary-empire-builder-genghis-khan-s-legacy/a-79697112--675146225486.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/brutal-revolutionary-empire-builder-genghis-khan-s-legacy/a-79697112?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Berlin is host to a major exhibition on the founder of the Mongol Empire and his successors. Here's how Genghis Khan disrupted the course of history.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Why malnutrition can go unnoticed, and what that means for our health</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9f5f33f62321cd1c01e9f85a134aa784b03938f9a6024894baefb6b69d8ec56b</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T05:48:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/why-malnutrition-can-go-unnoticed-and-what-that-means-for-our-health/a-79871016--a7247c0af1e5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/why-malnutrition-can-go-unnoticed-and-what-that-means-for-our-health/a-79871016?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">More than 2 billion people worldwide suffer from nutrient deficiencies, most of them in the Global South. The health consequences can be devastating.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Exercise coaching boosts colon cancer survival</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:551d8e691fe9dbe044960196f6afeeaf3d138f7ace070b563612e87fea3d7a1b</id><updated>2026-11-12T07:26:31Z</updated><published>2026-11-06T11:11:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/exercise-coaching-boosts-colon-cancer-survival/a-79856133--6184ce6c4193.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/exercise-coaching-boosts-colon-cancer-survival/a-79856133?maca=en-rss-en-cul-2090-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Evidence suggests exercise can keep more cancer patients alive. But what does "living longer" actually mean, and who does it apply to?</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
